            <ArticleTitle>Superficial Femoral Artery Stent Disruption Treated by Peripheral Endograft.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1661.e5-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.avsg.2015.06.085</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0890-5096(15)00622-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case of superficial femoral artery (SFA) stent fractures (SF) with atypical symptoms and site of disruption. Patient was hospitalized for sudden onset of right thigh pain, nonrelated to steno-obstructive disease. Preoperative ultrasound suspected and computed tomographic angiography (CTA) confirmed multiple proximal SFA SFs with concurrent pseudoaneurysms. A peripheral endograft was deployed covering the entire SFA, achieving a complete &quot;relining&quot; with exclusion of the pseudoaneurysm. Pain disappeared and postoperative control demonstrated good patency of the SFA. After 1 month, patient reported no further events and CTA revealed patency of the endograft and exclusion of the pseudoaneurysm. At 1 year follow-up, Viabahn is patent with no further symptoms reported by the patient.</AbstractText>
